      "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/595222a0a6987c1fae1a7b245d2936eba08312357015cc6f78c66eb60f942707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$",
